module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_capacity_screen_counts_a_population_it_never_identifies

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_capacity_screen_counts_a_population_it_never_identifies: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_capacity_screen_counts_a_population_it_never_identifies" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a capacity screen counts a population it never identifies** (INVALID STATE: a screen whose job is to rule out shapes too large for the resources available compares a requirement to the CARDINALITY of a resource population -- `needed <= host_count`, `replicas <= seat_count` -- while the corpus already carries that population's MEMBERS and already carries, in other authorities, which members are spoken for. The screen is then arithmetic over a number that no longer denotes what it is being read as: the count of resources that EXIST, standing in for the count of resources AVAILABLE. ",

    "HARM, AND IT IS ONE-DIRECTIONAL, WHICH IS WHY IT IS WORSE THAN AN ORDINARY WRONG ANSWER. The error can only ever be generous. A count of existing members is greater than or equal to the count of free ones, so every shape the screen wrongly admits is a shape too big to place, and no shape is ever wrongly refused. The one screen whose entire purpose is ruling shapes OUT therefore fails open, and it fails open silently, because a scalar has no field in which to carry the fact that some of what it counts is taken. What arrives downstream is a well-formed answer -- a selected topology, a receipt, a committed decision -- whose infeasibility is discovered by whoever tries to place it, at actuation, on machines someone else is using. ",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT, AND IT SEPARATES THIS FROM ORDINARY MISSING MODELLING: THE IDENTITIES WERE ALREADY MODELLED, IN AUTHORITIES THE SCREEN'S OWN MODULE COULD REACH. This is not a fleet whose membership nobody wrote down. The count is a SUMMARY of facts already present, authored beside them, and it went stale the moment any of them said something the summary cannot express. That is why the repair is never 'subtract the taken ones from the count': it is to delete the scalar and derive the SET, because a count recomputed from the same authorities is the same defect with a fresher number, and the next commitment fact that appears will not fit in it either. ",

    "**SPECIMEN, gunbc 2026-09-11 (found by review, #11098 section 14). IT IS LIVE AS THIS ROW IS FILED, AND THAT IS THE FIRST THING THE ROW HAS TO SAY.** gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection CARRIES `spark_fleet_snapshot` with `host_count: 8` and no host identities, and `fleet_fits` screens a candidate with `hosts_consumed(c) <= fleet.host_count`; the topology domain is generated over the same scalar. Eight DGX Spark units exist. FIVE of them are spoken for by authorities the screen never reads: srv5-srv8 are the four hosts of cage 1 (gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed), the one serving group gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired claims; srv5 and srv6 additionally carry gunbc.spark.cell_role assignments; and srv9 carries the GLM canary's placement claim, which gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment states outright -- the canary is serving there and nothing in this repository launched it through the pair route. So the screen admitted an eight-host shape onto a fleet with THREE free hosts, and the answer it produced was not merely unlucky -- it was unplaceable. AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS RECEIPT SAID FOUR FREE HOSTS AND CALLED CAGE 1 'the four-rank-by-two deployment', AND BOTH WERE WRONG IN THE SAME DIRECTION: it counted only the commitment causes the first repair happened to model, and it borrowed a candidate label -- tp4_x2 is TWO four-rank replicas, eight hosts -- for a four-host network group, conflating the cage with a deployment shape nobody established runs on it. A row about a screen that counts what it has not identified is the worst place to name a population it has not enumerated. ",

    "**THE SECOND SPECIMEN IS IN THE SAME REPAIR AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW IS NOT ONLY ABOUT COUNTS.** gunbc.spark.vllm_runtime_image_build had already derived the correct SET for its own question (where may a container build run), privately, as `admissible_spark_build_hosts`. So the corpus simultaneously held a scalar that was generous and a set that was better, neither aware of the other. The scalar said eight; that private set said four; they differ by FOUR, and an earlier revision of this receipt said two -- a number that described neither pair, because it came from comparing an intermediate draft of the new derivation against the private one rather than from anything the corpus held. Nor was the private set RIGHT: it read claimed serving groups and held reservations and missed the serving-arm placement, so it too answered four where three was the truth. A capacity scalar is therefore also a fork detector, and the sharper lesson is what the fork's better half still got wrong: where one consumer summarises a population it does not own, another usually derives it, they do not agree, and the derived one is not thereby correct -- it is merely correct about the causes IT models. What made the difference legible was not the count but bringing both under one authority where a missing cause has one place to be added. The first half of the repair (#11107) adds the shared authority, cutting the derivation to one authority, gunbc.spark.host_commitment, and moved the BUILD lane and gunbc.spark.v41_runtime_image_probe onto it. The SELECTION screen does not consume it yet: that cutover remains a follow-up outside that change, so what is discharged there is the fork, not the fail-open. ",

    "WHAT SEPARATES THIS FROM ITS NEAREST NEIGHBOURS. `roster_is_its_own_denominator` is a roster measured against itself; here there is no roster at the screen at all, only its size. `absorbing_fallback` widens on a failure to compute; this screen never fails -- it computes confidently over the wrong denotation. `selection_view_read_as_population` mistakes a filtered view for the whole; here the whole IS what is counted, and the mistake is that the whole is not the available. The shared consequence is an answer that outruns its evidence; the distinguishing remedy is that only this one is fixed by deleting a number. ",

    "**RUNG, AS ESTABLISHED BY EXECUTED EVIDENCE AND NOT AS INTENDED: OUTSIDE THE LADDER, TODAY, AT THE SPECIMEN SITE.** The screen accepts and answers, and the wrongness is silent until placement. Nothing in #11107 changes that: it adds the authority and moves the build lane, and the selection screen still compares against the scalar, so a row claiming the site had climbed would be citing the path that moved while the path that matters stayed silent -- DESIGN section 4b's rung inflation, which is the fabricated-plausible-output failure turned on the corpus's description of itself. **An earlier revision of this row DID claim rung 4 at this site while the scalar was untouched two directories away, and a reviewer caught it (cursor, review 64102, on #11107); that is recorded here rather than quietly corrected, because a ledger row filed to catch over-reading is the worst possible place for an over-read.** WHAT THE CEILING AT THIS SITE WILL BE once the cutover lands: 4 for the count-versus-set confusion and no higher -- with the field holding identities and no cardinality field beside them, `needed <= count_of_existing` has nothing to be written against, because a consumer must name members. It is NOT rung 4 for the underlying question of whether the commitment authorities are COMPLETE; a host spoken for by something nobody has modelled is outside the modelled guarantee and stays there, which is a boundary rather than a rung. ",

    "**NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER FOR THE SPECIMEN SITE, named as the capability and not as an artifact: gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection's fleet snapshot carries host identities with NO cardinality field beside them, and every consumer that screened against the count -- `fleet_fits` and the topology domain generator both -- names members instead.** SUFFICIENT FOR: a candidate whose shape needs more hosts than are admissible has no admitting arm to reach, and a receipt cannot be minted against a set it did not read. A trigger naming only `fleet_fits` would be satisfied while the topology domain still enumerated shapes off the scalar. ",

    "**NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER FOR THE CLASS, as distinct from the specimen site above: a lens over the accepted corpus that flags a comparison between a requirement and a length-or-count field whose module also reaches an authority enumerating that population's members.** That is decidable -- both operands and the import closure are in the Node tree -- so this is DESIGN section 5's wall-after-grounding rather than a ratchet, and the class stays at 1 corpus-wide until such a lens executes. Naming a narrower trigger (this module's repair, or a review checklist) would satisfy itself while the class stayed alive everywhere else. ",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, ONE QUESTION ASKED OF ANY CAPACITY COMPARISON: does the number on the right-hand side count things whose identities this corpus already carries? If yes, the comparison is a summary standing where a set belongs, and the follow-up is the one that finds the real defect -- which authorities already say some of those members are spoken for, and does anything join them? The behavioural tell is a field named `*_count` on a snapshot type whose siblings are all measurements of a member (bytes per host, cores per node), because that is the shape where one scalar quietly changes category from a measurement to a population.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
